  <data name="Rule.cpu.high.Name" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sustained high CPU</value></data>
  <data name="Rule.cpu.high.Rationale" xml:space="preserve"><value>Global CPU above 90 % for 30 s: foreground work is likely to be slowed down.</value></data>
  <data name="Rule.cpu.high.FalsePositives" xml:space="preserve"><value>Builds, compressions, games and video exports legitimately keep the CPU busy.</value></data>
  <data name="Rule.mem.available.low.Name" xml:space="preserve"><value>Very low available memory</value></data>
  <data name="Rule.mem.available.low.Rationale" xml:space="preserve"><value>Available memory (standby + free + zero pages) under 512 MB: Windows must page out to satisfy new allocations. An absolute amount, not a percentage of RAM, because a high 'memory in use' percentage alone is normal with a large cache.</value></data>
  <data name="Rule.mem.available.low.FalsePositives" xml:space="preserve"><value>On machines with little RAM, the threshold may need to be lowered.</value></data>
  <data name="Rule.mem.commit.high.Name" xml:space="preserve"><value>Commit charge near its limit</value></data>
  <data name="Rule.mem.commit.high.Rationale" xml:space="preserve"><value>Committed memory above 90 % of the commit limit: allocations can fail once the limit is reached.</value></data>
  <data name="Rule.mem.commit.high.FalsePositives" xml:space="preserve"><value>With a growable page file the limit is soft and Windows may extend it.</value></data>
  <data name="Rule.disk.latency.high.Name" xml:space="preserve"><value>High disk latency</value></data>
  <data name="Rule.disk.latency.high.Rationale" xml:space="preserve"><value>Average time per disk transfer above 50 ms for 30 s: programs waiting on the disk feel slow. Measured over all physical disks.</value></data>
  <data name="Rule.disk.latency.high.FalsePositives" xml:space="preserve"><value>Hard disks and USB drives under sequential load commonly exceed 50 ms; the threshold suits SSDs.</value></data>
